\DOC LOCAL_MUL_P256K1_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_P256K1_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal across one inlined
modular-multiplication subroutine call, replacing the current symbolic state by
a successor state and adding an assumption characterising the newly written
result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int}, the instruction-offset/index adjustment used
when locating the call site (the same constant in all observed traces). The
second is a {string list} naming the three operand locations of the
multiplication — the destination buffer and the two sources — which selects the
stored values the new assumptions talk about. The goal must be a predicate of
the form "{eventually} ... holds of state {s}"; the tactic rewrites it to the
same predicate applied to a fresh successor state {s'} and rebuilds the
assumption list for that state. Every assumption mentioning the old state — the
program-counter value, the program-loaded fact, register contents,
memory-contents facts, and the accumulated frame/{MAYCHANGE} relation from the
initial state — is discharged and re-added with the new state name, the
program-counter assumption being updated to the new offset and the frame
relation extended by the destination location just written. Additionally, a
fresh variable {z} (named from the first element of the string list) is
introduced by an equation defining it as the modular product of the values held
in the two source locations, accompanied by a memory-contents assumption that
the destination holds {z}; equations established earlier are retained
unchanged.
{
   A, {assumptions about s} ?- eventually ... s
   ======================================================  LOCAL_MUL_P256K1_TAC n ["dest"; "src1"; "src2"]
   A, {same assumptions restated for s', pc at new offset,
       frame relation extended by dest},
      z = (value at src1) * (value at src2) (mod ...),
      memory at dest = z
    ?- eventually ... s'
}

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this same single kind of transformation, on goals of
this {eventually} shape.

\ENDDOC
